Semantic Surface Area

A Pantheon whitepaper on using, predicting, and orchestrating meaning

Version 1.0 · November 2026

Abstract

A phrase can stay verbatim while its function changes across speakers, media, communities, and decades. A community can also change which meaning it rewards before most of its members change their minds. This paper proposes an operational framework for both phenomena. Its unit of study is an encounter between a signal and a situated audience. Its outputs are a forecast of likely readings, an account of how those readings travel, and a sequence of interventions that can be tested and revised. The framework is a design proposal. The examples and numerical results are illustrative.

1. What has surface area?

Semantic surface area is the set of consequential readings a signal makes available across a specified range of contexts. Consequential readings change what people feel, infer, repeat, or do. A single syllable can have a large surface. A long technical specification can have a narrow one.

“Hold the line” can be a phone instruction, a defensive instruction in a game, or an appeal for solidarity. A sharp delivery can make the last reading feel like a command. A tired delivery can make it a plea. On a cropped poster, the scene that disambiguated the phrase disappears. Each of those is a different encounter with the same words.

|Layer              |What to inspect                                       |What can change                                                    |
|-------------------|------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------|
|Words              |Literal reference, idiom, metaphor, grammatical agency|What people think the message is about                             |
|Sound              |Pitch, stress, pacing, silence, timbre                |How a listener reads stance, urgency, or sincerity                 |
|Situation          |Speaker, relationship, setting, preceding event       |Whether the phrase is heard as care, permission, pressure, or irony|
|Circulation        |Captions, clips, remixes, who relays it               |Which cues survive the handoff                                     |
|Social consequences|What earns laughter, status, access, or sanctions     |Which readings people repeat or enact in public                    |

A phrase can be richly ambiguous across many settings and highly predictable inside one. Measure breadth across contexts and ambiguity within a context separately. Always name the contexts sampled. A universal surface-area score would hide which communities and media the measurement actually covered.

2. From little zeitgeists to the big one

A local zeitgeist consists of conventions and expectations people repeatedly encounter in a group. The larger zeitgeist is partly formed by which local meanings get repeated across boundaries and which gain institutional consequences. A joke can become a slogan, a slogan can become a reporting category, and that category can change what gets funded. At each step, something is carried forward and something can be lost.

Track four quantities separately:

|Quantity         |Example of observable evidence                            |
|-----------------|----------------------------------------------------------|
|Exposure         |Distinct people or groups that encounter the expression   |
|Interpretation   |What people say it means in an unprompted paraphrase      |
|Public expression|Which readings people share, quote, or visibly endorse    |
|Adoption         |What people repeatedly do, including after attention fades|

A fifth quantity, private agreement, requires its own evidence, such as an anonymous survey. Inferring it from public posts confuses visibility, permission, and belief. A cultural snapshot should report reach and prevalence separately. One reading could dominate a feed while another dominates a representative survey.

The DNA analogy is most productive as a question about expression. Ask which readings are already present, what conditions make one visible, and how repeated expression changes the conditions for the next round. Communities can also invent readings, assign new rewards, and change the distribution channels themselves.

3. How to predict it

1. Specify the encounter and decision. Name the phrase, audio or visual treatment, speaker, audience, channel, and time window. State the action the forecast will inform.
2. Map readings from observed language. Ask a small sample to paraphrase freely before showing any labels. Record intended, adjacent, adverse, and unclear readings. If one response can hold multiple meanings, code a primary reading for the distribution and retain secondary tags.
3. Start with local baselines. Estimate the distribution for each audience and medium with counts and denominators. Use broad ranges when evidence is thin. Borrow from similar contexts explicitly and mark the forecast as provisional. A model can suggest hypotheses. Estimates of human reactions need human observations.
4. Test what moves the distribution. Randomly assign comparable participants to versions where feasible. Change one cue at a time, or cross two cues in a factorial test. Record exposure, interpretation, voluntary relay, and action separately. Retest after a delay to check what persists.
5. Forecast the handoff. Identify who might relay the signal, what trust they bring, and what they will likely cut. Test a screenshot, silent clip, and spoken retelling. Report spillover and drift by audience. Where groups contaminate one another, randomize by group or treat the comparison as observational.
6. Log and score the forecast. Freeze predictions before results arrive. Compare with a baseline that repeats the last observed rates. Check whether outcomes assigned similar probabilities happen at about those rates over repeated tests. Keep misses and revise the assumptions that produced them.

Minimal quantitative model

Let $p(m\mid c)$ be the probability of the primary reading $m$ given the encounter $c$. Estimate it from coded responses. If codes are mutually exclusive, their probabilities sum to one. Record missing responses separately and report the response rate. A conditional entropy, $H(M\mid c)=-\sum_m p(m\mid c)\log_2 p(m\mid c)$, summarizes how diffuse the readings are within one setting. Report the distribution alongside the summary, since a harmful interpretation and a playful reinterpretation carry different costs.

For a forecast across contexts, use $p(m)=\sum_c w_c p(m\mid c)$. Set the weights $w_c$ to match the question. Equal weights compare local worlds on equal footing. Estimated audience weights forecast what exposed people are likely to hear. Future weights are themselves uncertain because relay and amplification can shift them.

To evaluate a probabilistic forecast, use mean multiclass Brier score, $\sum_m(p_m-y_m)^2$ per encounter, where $y_m=1$ for the observed primary reading and zero otherwise. Lower is better. A forecast of .70 for the intended reading and .30 for an alternative scores .18 when the intended reading occurs and .98 when the alternative occurs. Evaluate on new encounters. A before-and-after movement by itself leaves open changes in audience mix and outside events.

4. How to orchestrate it

Orchestration gives different surfaces different jobs. Decide what must travel intact, where people may reinterpret it, and what material conditions make the message credible.

|Lever                  |Use it to                                                              |Check                                                               |
|-----------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------|
|Wording                |Fix the action, permission, or promise in plain language               |Ask for an unprompted paraphrase and the next action                |
|Prosody and image      |Carry tone and emotional intent                                        |Compare audio, captions, and muted playback                         |
|Sequence               |Ground the meaning with an example before compressing it into a phrase |Test whether the short form still works after the introduction fades|
|Messenger              |Let people familiar with each audience express it in their own language|Ask recipients what survived and what changed                       |
|Distribution           |Place the message where it can be understood and discussed             |Sample exposed people beyond the visible commenters                 |
|Incentives and practice|Make the stated norm observable in how people are treated              |Inspect decisions, repeated behavior, and reports of friction       |
|Correction             |Repair consequential misunderstandings at the point they appear        |Test whether the correction reaches the same people                 |

Use the narrowest surface for instructions and commitments. Give stories, music, humor, and rituals wider surfaces when varied interpretations are part of the value. Invite participation openly, explain material commitments plainly, and make declining easy. Use aggregate observations and opt-in feedback where practical.

5. Worked demonstration — “Run at your own pace”

A run club wants first-timers to know they can join at a conversational pace and still have company. The phrase alone also permits “you’re on your own” and “the group says that, but everyone races.” The social setting produces those readings as much as the words do.

Design. Test a short invitation against the same invitation plus “A volunteer stays with the last group. Walking breaks are welcome.” Arrange a volunteer before making the promise. Use the same voice, timing, and imagery for the wording comparison. In a later test, vary the delivery while holding the copy fixed. Ask participants to explain what would happen if they fell behind. Then route the compact phrase through one partner group and test its retelling.

|Primary reading                 |Short copy forecast|Concrete promise forecast|
|--------------------------------|------------------:|------------------------:|
|I will have company at my pace  |50%                |75%                      |
|I may run alone if I fall behind|30%                |10%                      |
|Pace will be judged informally  |15%                |10%                      |
|Other or unclear                |5%                 |5%                       |

These are hypothetical forecasts. If 29 of 40 respondents in the second arm give the intended reading versus 20 of 40 in the first, the observed difference is 22.5 percentage points. Under independent random assignment, an approximate 95% interval for that difference is about 2 to 43 points. Report the wide interval and repeat with a fresh cohort. The test measures understanding under its viewing conditions. Measure actual attendance and whether people who fall behind have company in a later field pilot.

Orchestration. Give the public invite a memorable phrase, the signup page the literal promise, and the volunteers a clear role. Have the organizer explain the arrangement at the start. At the finish, ask first-timers privately what happened. If paraphrases are accurate and attendance is low, examine scheduling, location, and signup friction. If first-timers arrive and end up alone, fix the practice. If a partner’s repost drops the promise and reintroduces doubt, give them a compact version that carries both the phrase and the concrete detail.

6. A Pantheon application — the unseen uses of an answer

A transcript records a signal and some of its context. One person can use that same answer as a draft, a sounding board, a source of language for a story, or a record of an idea they plan to revisit. The answer may also prompt an idea because of a gap the reader noticed. Record observed use only when the person supplies it. Keep inferred uses as hypotheses and preserve the original exchange so a later reader can recover what each interpretation was responding to.

For the Silence Guitar, take one phrase and build three versions with identical wording. Change only stress and pause. Then render each as audio, as plain text, and as a caption that adds scene context. Ask listeners and readers to classify what the speaker was doing and what they would do next. The comparison shows where meaning resides in the words, where performance shifts it, and where another medium must supply what went missing.

Reusable experiment record

|Field                 |What to write down                                        |
|----------------------|----------------------------------------------------------|
|Signal and version    |Exact wording, audio or visual cue, date                  |
|Context               |Speaker, shared assumptions, audience, medium             |
|Fixed meaning         |The promise or fact that should survive                   |
|Readings and forecasts|Primary codes, probabilities, evidence, uncertainty       |
|Proposed change       |Cue, sequence, messenger, or practice being changed       |
|Outcome               |Exposure, paraphrase, relay, action, and delayed follow-up|
|Decision              |Continue, revise, or stop, with reason and next test      |

Research foundations

• Clark, H. H., and Brennan, S. E. (1991). Grounding in communication. Provides the account of common ground and how different media change the work required to establish understanding. Chapter
• Salganik, M. J., Dodds, P. S., and Watts, D. J. (2006). Experimental study of inequality and unpredictability in an artificial cultural market. Greater social influence increased inequality and unpredictability in the study’s music market, motivating caution about forecasting which version will spread. Science
• Centola, D. (2010). The spread of behavior in an online social network experiment. In the experimental setting, social reinforcement and network structure affected behavioral diffusion. This motivates testing adoption and relay as distinct outcomes. Science